//===- UnaryPlusIsIdentity.pdll - the first pattern over OUR dialect ------===//
//
// `+x` is `x` once admission has proved x a number. This type-preserving
// rewrite runs before native carrier lowering.
//
// This rule preserves CTJS types: it replaces a `!ctjs.value` result with a
// `!ctjs.value` operand before retype(). The remaining scalar lowering builds
// EmitC operations after retype() has converted every value from the solver
// lattice. At that point a `ctjs.unary` can have an f64 operand where its ODS
// declares `CTJS_ValueType`, so it is not a suitable stage for this driver.
//
// Analysis access itself is supported. MLIR 23 registers external PDLL
// constraints and rewrites as std::function, which can capture invocation-local
// analysis and side tables. Symbolic/Rewrite.cpp uses that API with callbacks
// whose lifetime ends before the captured analysis. Inline native snippets in
// a .pdll file do not acquire pass locals implicitly; imported declarations
// plus explicit callback registration provide that connection without globals.
//
// WHAT MAKES IT SOUND IS WHERE IT RUNS, not what it matches. `+x` in general
// is ToNumber: it can re-enter user code through valueOf and it can produce a
// different value ("1" becomes 1). It is the identity only on a value already
// proved numeric, which is exactly what admission::op()'s
//   case UnaryKind::Plus: return numeric(u.getOperand(), "unary");
// establishes - so this pattern is run by lowering::lower(), which is called
// for accepted functions and nothing else. That is the same guarantee the C++
// arm it replaces had; it is written down here because a pattern file looks
// context-free and this one is not.
//
// AND IF IT DOES NOT FIRE, THE BUILD SAYS SO. PDL has no diagnostic on a
// non-match, so replace() keeps an arm for UnaryKind::Plus that calls
// report_fatal_error naming this file. A silently non-firing pattern is the
// failure mode this project cares most about; it is not available here.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// OUR OWN ODS, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE FILE. With it, `op<ctjs.unary>` is
// checked for operand and result shape against CTJSOps.td - naming two
// operands is a hard error quoting the record - and `Op<ctjs.unary>` in the
// constraint below translates to the concrete C++ class. It does NOT check the
// NAME: `op<ctjs.binry>` is exit 0 and silence, and that is DELIBERATE - the
// reference supports unregistered operations on purpose. And it does not make
// `attr<"#ctjs.unary_kind<plus>">` parse - that prints "'none' attribute
// created with unregistered dialect", exits 0, and drops the constraint, which
// IS a trap. utils/pdll-strict.sh fails the build on both; the kind test below
// is native because of the second.
#include "ctcompile/CTJS/IR/CTJSOps.td"

//===- The native constraint ----------------------------------------------===//

// THE KIND, IN C++, BECAUSE THE ATTRIBUTE LITERAL IS NOT AVAILABLE. Writing
// `op<ctjs.unary> {kind = attr<"#ctjs.unary_kind<plus>">}` compiles and emits
// a pattern with NO constraint on `kind` at all, which would rewrite `-x` and
// `!x` to their operands. A native constraint is not a workaround for that: it
// is better, because `UnaryKind::Pluss` is a C++ compile error where the
// literal's typo is silence.
//
// ONE CALL INTO A NAMED FUNCTION, which is the policy's rule for native
// bodies - inline C++ in a pattern file is not clang-formatted, not covered by
// -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Wconversion, and not separately testable. `rewriter`
// is threaded through because mlir-pdll generates the wrapper with that
// parameter whether the body wants it or not, and an unused parameter is a
// build failure here.
//
// `Op<ctjs.unary>` AND NOT A BARE `Op`, which is not decoration: a NAMED
// operation constraint whose ODS has been included translates to the CONCRETE
// C++ class, so isUnaryPlus receives a `ctcompile::ctjs::UnaryOp` and needs no
// dyn_cast. A bare `Op` would hand it an `::mlir::Operation *` and put the
// narrowing back in C++, where it is one more thing to get wrong.
Constraint IsUnaryPlus(op: Op<ctjs.unary>) [{
  return ::ctcompile::ctnative::pdll::isUnaryPlus(rewriter, op);
}];

//===- The pattern --------------------------------------------------------===//

Pattern UnaryPlusIsIdentity {
  let root = op<ctjs.unary>(operand: Value);
  IsUnaryPlus(root);
  replace root with operand;
}
